WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.000
Movies are like games of Jenga: if just one
piece is out of place, the entire thing collapses,

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.320
no matter how well built the rest of it is.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.710
Even movies that otherwise were great have
been ruined by a bad decision.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.650
Here's a look at movies that were destroyed
by just one scene...

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.120
The Dark Knight Rises

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.740
The Dark Knight Rises was full of little head-scratching
moments, such as "If the villain's plan is

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.599
to blow up Gotham after holding it hostage,
why does he wait all those months to do it?"

00:00:23.599 --> 00:00:27.580
Most of these you can just let slide, but
the reveal of Joseph Gordon-Levitt's real

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:28.580
identity?

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.599
That's...that's just too much.

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:31.379
"Nothing here."

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.000
"Try my legal name."

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.840
"You should use your full name, I like that
name—Robin."

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.100
You know, Robin!

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.620
Of course, in the comics, Robin's real name
is Dick.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:46.620
Or Jason.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.620
Or Tim.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:48.620
Or Damien.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:49.620
Or even Stephanie.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.170
But apparently any of those names would have
been too subtle for movie audiences, so they

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:54.750
hit you over the head instead.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.140
It's like revealing this dude's name is "John
Aquaman" or something.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.199
Interstellar

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:03.640
At the climax of Christopher Nolan's sci-fi
epic Interstellar, Matthew McConaughey's pilot

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.260
character falls into a black hole and lands
inside a far-flung future device that lets

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.850
him send cryptic messages to his daughter
in the past.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:16.860
Or in other words, he has to use some hippy-dippy
Morse Code machine to save the future using

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.620
— that's right — the power of love.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:26.360
"No, no, no, no, Murph!! MURPH!!!! No! No!"

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:27.590
Really?

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:28.820
Love?

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.130
That's the secret to unlocking the mysteries
of the time-space continuum?

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.200
"Great Scott!"

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.910
Why didn't they just end the movie with this
song, then?

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:42.410
"...but it might just save your life, that's
the power of love."

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.340
Prometheus

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.540
There was a minute there where it looked like
Ridley Scott's Prometheus was going to be

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.740
one of the coolest movies ever made.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.520
But what it turned out to be was a slow exercise
in cluelessness.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.770
Plenty of fans either love it or hate it,
but it's hard to argue about that one, sequence

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.560
where the characters have to avoid being crushed
by a rolling spaceship.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.190
It looks tense, exciting, thrilling even — right
up until you ask yourself: why didn't they

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.640
just move to the side instead of trying to
outrun it?

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.550
In fact, the main character lives simply by
rolling three feet.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.560
Now if only she had told her buddy to do the
same thing, maybe we wouldn't have left the

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.480
theater feeling profoundly stupider.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.850
12 Years a Slave

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.350
Throughout the majority of 12 Years a Slave,
the story of Solomon Northup, a free man who

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:25.750
was kidnapped and forced into slavery, is
artfully told through brutal visuals and compelling

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.030
performances.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.110
Then Brad Pitt shows up.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.910
And he speaks, using whatever that accent
this was supposed to be, with his Brad Pitt

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.250
acting and his Brad Pitt face:

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:35.900
"Freedom is everything.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.780
The fact that I can even walk out here tomorrow
brings me great pleasure."

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.580
Uh...start walking, Brad.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.260
That would give us great pleasure.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.430
Let the Right One In

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:50.730
Let the Right One In is a rare kind of horror
movie, a haunting character study with few

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.840
loud moments or overt scares.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.660
It is, simply put, an excellent, creepy flick.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:00.010
But it does have one massive, unintentional
belly laugh moment that kinda ruins it...

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:11.610
(cats roaring and hissing)

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.940
Here's a free tip to any future horror movie
directors.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:18.110
If you want to keep your audience terrified,
and on the edge of their seats, just skip

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:19.890
the evil attack kittens.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.010
Kittens can't really pull off the evil thing,
y'know?

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.700
They're just too damn cute!

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.280
"I'm going to kill you!"

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.150
Star Wars: Special Edition

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:40.830
In 1997, fans were excited at the chance to
see the remastered Star Wars trilogy on the

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:41.830
big screen.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.920
And then...this happened.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.640
"I bet you have."

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.740
In an instant, people realized George Lucas
hadn't improved the series with the Special

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:51.740
Editions.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:52.740
He'd ruined it.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.430
The collective scream of distraught fans is
still reverberating around the universe.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:59.120
"I felt a great disturbance in the Force.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:03.940
As if millions of voices suddenly cried out
in terror and were suddenly silenced."

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:07.280
Well...maybe not exactly "silenced."

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.080
Return of the Jedi: Special Edition

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:13.920
Now, you can almost understand the thought
process behind having Greedo shoot first.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.169
But for every revision Lucas has explained,
there are at least three others made to the

00:04:18.169 --> 00:04:21.059
trilogy are just random and bizarre.

00:04:21.059 --> 00:04:25.770
And none of them are more random, or more
bizarre, than the insanely stupid "Jedi Rocks"

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.099
dance number in the middle of Return of the
Jedi.

00:04:28.099 --> 00:04:29.939
Prepare yourselves:

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.100
"I have a very bad feeling about this."

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:38.860
(horrible music playing)

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.420
"No!"

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.800
"Nooooo!

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:45.780
No!"

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.220
"No, no, no!"

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.719
"I don't really have a lot of awards, to be
very honest with you."

00:04:52.719 --> 00:04:53.819
Thanks for watching!

00:04:53.819 --> 00:04:56.340
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.610
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

